{{ macros.section(2, "Signals and Supervisors", id="signals-and-supervisors") }}

!!! danger "Advanced topic"
    New users can safely skip this.  **Working with signals (or understanding them) is not required for most use-cases of `compose.mk`,** especially if you're using it as a library.   

Every direct invocation of `compose.mk` optionally gets a "supervisor" process that allows for enhanced use (abuse?) of POSIX signals. Amongst other use-cases, you can use this pattern to yield control to another process in the middle of target-processing, or to implement entrance/exit handlers for `make`, in `make`.

[Tool mode invocations]({{_}}/tool-overview/#tool-wrappers) implicitly involve supervisors and signals, and signals also enable certain experimental features, and in principle the supervisor can be extended with new entry/exit hooks.

Power users might be interested to understand the caveats and limitations around this, and anyone looking to work deeply on `compose.mk` itself might need to grok the implementation.

### Background & Motivation
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Without [forking make](https://remake.readthedocs.io/en/latest/), there's no simple method to get hooks into the default way that it handles interrupts and signals.  But why would you want to anyway?  Most of the time this occurs to people [it's about cleanup](https://www.gnu.org/software/make/manual/html_node/Interrupts.html), but `compose.mk` doesn't exactly look like traditional use-cases.  

For `compose.mk` specifically, signals come up when we need to stop `make` from processing the command line input, and pass control over to a new process.  But why?  Well, short-circuiting the default CLI option parsing for `make` *really useful*.  Especially since `compose.mk` allows us to "wrap" lots of containerized tools, we want to be able to proxy arguments over to those tools without `make` being greedy about parsing everything.  

Here's a few typical examples of why you might want **individual targets to essentially be able to consume the rest of the command line.**

Pulling that off means the supervisor stops `make` at just the right moment and hands the raw command line to a fresh process.  That hand-off is exactly the job of the supervisor's [bootloader](#bootloader) and the [trampoline]({{_}}/runtime/#flat-dispatch) dispatch loop it hosts, both introduced further down this page and covered in full on the [Runtime]({{_}}/runtime) page.  Keep them in mind: the rest of this page builds toward that machinery.

> **Typical cases.**  Several commands need a target to read the whole command line rather than let `make` parse it:
> {: .cmk-card }
>
> * The [help command]({{_}}/cli-help) has an optional 2nd argument, a target, which must **not** be parsed by `make`, as that would actually execute it!
> * The [`loadf` command]({{_}}/loadf): the 2nd argument, a filename, must **not** be parsed as a Makefile target (no such target exists).
> * The [wrappers for `jq` and `jb`]({{_}}/tool-overview/#proxy-wrappers) likewise need to read the whole command-line.
>
> ```bash {.cli_example}
> # Several example invocations where arguments are NOT targets.
> $ ./compose.mk help docker.image.run
> $ ./compose.mk loadf demos/data/docker-compose.yml
> $ echo '{"foo":"bar"}' | ./compose.mk jq .foo
> $ ./compose.mk jb foo=bar
> ```

### Inheritance & Interpreters
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Projects that are extending `compose.mk` might be interested in *inheriting* the capabilities for signals and supervisors.  The simplest reason you might want to do this is just for the sake of bringing [CLI help]({{_}}/cli-help) to your own project, *i.e. targets that actually support positional arguments,* but [the advanced examples](#advanced-examples) on this page hint at other possibilities.  

{{ macros.admonition("warning", "Signals aren't inherited through `include`", "For [technical reasons](#implementation-details) there is no way to inherit signal capabilities by simply `include`-ing `compose.mk` from your project Makefiles.  The work-around is to set up `compose.mk` itself as the interpreter; and for an interpreter *and* a compiler, see instead [the compiler docs](" ~ _ ~ "/cmk/compiler).") }}

This pattern is an advanced topic, but it is both stable and portable, even if the semantics are strange enough to take some getting used to.

The end-game for this style of extension is the [trampoline]({{_}}/runtime/#flat-dispatch) dispatch loop, where re-shaping the remaining command line becomes a first-class control primitive.  But we can build up to that gradually, starting from the simplest case: short-circuiting `make`'s default command line processing with [`mk.yield`](#positional-arguments-yield).

#### Positional Arguments & Yield
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

Let's start with something simple.  To demonstrate short-circuiting the default command line processing of `make`, we'll try to create a clone of the `ls` command.  

In the code, the differences start right away.  See the shebang at the top of the following file which uses {{site.api_link('mk.interpret', mkdocs)}} ?

{{site.embed_mirror('demos/interpreter.mk', 'demos/cmk/interpreter.cmk', github=github)}}

Using `compose.mk` to "interpret" your makefile isn't as magical as it sounds.  You can think of it as effectively *inserting **your** makefile at the bottom of the compose.mk source code,* then running the resulting temporary file as usual with `make`.  As a result of this, there are two important side-effects for project automation:

1. Automation has access to `compose.mk` API implicitly (regardless of any `include`)
1. Automation inherits the `compose.mk` extensions to signals

!!! closer_look "Closer Look"
    The next extremely magical line is the call to `mk.yield`, where the argument is the command line that you want to *yield execution to*.  We yield in this case to an `ls` invocation whose argument is a `MAKE_CLI` parameter-expansion, which expands to "the full command line, *after* the word `ls`".[^4]

At this point as long as the file is executable, then `./demos/interpreter.mk ls /` works as expected.  But the body of `__main__` demonstrates something surprising, which is that `mk.yield` really *does* drastically change the rest of program flow control afterwards.

You can skip the usage of `compose.mk` as an interpreter by invoking the file as `make -f demos/interpreter.mk ls /`, which *does work*, but includes a `Supervisor not found` message at the end.  And whereas `./demos/interpreter.mk` runs `__main__` and exits early with success, `make -f ./demos/interpreter.mk` fails because it does not exit early.

!!! danger "Caveat" 
    A subtle but **potentially serious issue** with this approach is that `./demos/interpreter.mk ls -la` works as expected, but `./demos/interpreter.mk ls --help` actually returns *help for make* and **not** *help for ls*.. implying that our intent to proxy the "rest" of the command line still has some limitations, and it actually depends on the content of that command line.

If you're only interested in proxy for **positional arguments** then this won't matter, meanwhile **option-proxy** works in some cases but must always be tested carefully.

#### Generic Option Proxy
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

The end of the last section hints at a basic problem with naive option-proxying.  What if we want `ls --help` to work, or to put it another way: **how do we stop make from eating our argv?**

The solution is the shell's *end of options* convention, a `--` separating the command from its arguments[^1]; `compose.mk` surfaces everything after it in the `MAKE_CLI_EXTRA` variable.[^2]

{{site.embed_mirror('demos/interpreter-2.mk', 'demos/cmk/interpreter-2.cmk', github=github)}}

As seen above, this sacrifices a little of the direct invocation's convenience, but the tool's own argv now reaches it: `ls -- --help` returns help for `ls` instead of for `make`.  

This is far more reliable than the last approach, **but still has known issues**: an option written with `=` (`--width=45`) breaks, while the space-separated form (`--width 45`) works.[^3] 

#### Advanced Examples
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

Of course, no one really cares about making an `ls` clone!  But besides the simple use-cases with targets that might want to accept pos-args and halt further execution, there are some other applications.

The example below demonstrates an embedded python-script that we'll run inside a container, and uses real option parsing.  

{{site.embed_mirror('demos/interpreter-3.mk', 'demos/cmk/interpreter-3.cmk', github=github)}}

Above we use the `mk.yield` macro with no arguments, which is equivalent to calling `mk.yield` with `true`, i.e. just stopping CLI parsing and exiting immediately. This is often sufficient and has the correct exit status, because if the line before it should fail, execution will stop anyway.

Anyway, using it looks like this:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./demos/interpreter-3.mk my-normal-target
```
```bash {.cli_output}
hello normal make target
```

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./demos/interpreter-3.mk 
```
```bash {.cli_output}
hello python opt parse
bar is set
foo is my.foo
```

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./demos/interpreter-3.mk script.run -- --help
```
```bash {.cli_output}
hello python opt parse
Usage: python_script [options] filename

Options:
  --version          show program's version number and exit
  -h, --help         show this help message and exit
  -f FOO, --foo=FOO  set foo option
  -b, --bar          set bar option
```

### Pre & Post Hooks 
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Pre- and post-hooks are a supervised-runtime feature: when the interpreter is used directly, `compose.mk` can automatically inject a `<target>.pre`/`<target>.post` hook around every target that runs via the command line.  Because they depend on the supervisor described on this page, they are documented alongside the rest of the runtime model; see [Pre & Post Hooks]({{_}}/runtime/#pre-post-hooks) on the Runtime page.

### At-Exit Handlers
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

To setup one or more exit handlers, try using the `CMK_POST` environment variable with space-separated target names.  This should run if `make` dies for any reason and even if it is killed, but cannot run if the wrapper process is killed.  The same handler list can also be set in-source via the `cmk_post` [pragma]({{_}}/cmk/pragmas/#pragma-lifecycle) or appended at parse-time with the `__cmk_post__.append` macro; all three surfaces accumulate.

At-exit handlers defined this way are called whenever `mk.super.exit` is called, which has the usual caveats that **you must be using a interpreter-style invocation,** and not running via `make -f ...`!  See the next section for more details.

The symmetric counterpart runs *before* the main pipeline: `CMK_PRE` (pragma `cmk_pre`, handle `__cmk_pre__`) names make-targets executed at the **boot stage** via `mk.super.boot`, with the same three-surface, accumulating semantics.  A failing pre-target aborts before the main pipeline (the at-exit handlers still run).

### Exact Exit Codes
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

GNU `make` collapses *any* recipe failure to exit code **2** at every sub-make boundary, so a meaningful code (say, a tool's exit status, or an unhandled-exception code) normally never reaches the OS.  The supervisor can carry an **exact** code to the top because the bash wrapper, not `make`, is the process that finally exits.

Use `mk.exit.code/<N>` to set it: it records the code out-of-band and fails *normally*, so the stack unwinds as usual (`flux.*.finally` and other cleanup still runs) and the top-level `./compose.mk` exits with exactly `<N>`:

```bash
$ ./your-program.cmk          # somewhere inside: $(call mk.exit.code,42)
$ echo $?
42
```

It's the "record-and-continue" cousin of [`mk.yield`](#positional-arguments-yield): where `mk.yield` short-circuits the whole stack immediately via signal (skipping `finally` arms), `mk.exit.code` unwinds so cleanup runs first.  Both need an interpreter-style invocation; without the supervisor there is no out-of-band channel and failures flatten to 2.  OS codes wrap mod 256, so keep the value in `0..255`.

Because the code is read at the very end, *swallowing* a failure with a trailing or-true or or-fallback guard continues execution but does **not** retract a code already recorded.  To truly recover (continue **and** exit 0), retract it with `mk.exit.clear`:

```make
risky:
	@${make} something || { handle_it ; ${make} mk.exit.clear ; }
```

`flux.try.except.finally` does this for you automatically: when its `except` arm *recovers* (succeeds), it calls `mk.exit.clear` and the construct returns 0.  Only hand-rolled error handling (outside the `flux.try.*` family) that intends to recover needs to call `mk.exit.clear` explicitly.

### Bootloader
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">
<a name=bootloader></a>

Just before it runs your program, the supervisor executes a small **bootloader**: the single, header-free extension point for supervisor behavior (both `compose.mk`'s own inlined loaders and a user-defined `CMK_BOOTLOADER` script).  Since it is a property of the supervisor and hosts the trampoline dispatch loop, it is documented with the rest of the runtime model; see [Bootloader]({{_}}/runtime/#bootloader) on the Runtime page.

### Implementation Details
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

If you're already very familiar with `make` and looking over the rest of this page, you might be wondering how this kind of semi-magical behavior can be achieved.   And since the answer to that question is frankly kind of ridiculous, this should probably be considered experimental =)  *With that sternly worded warning out of the way..* 

Signals and supervisors are implemented using a shebang hack[^5] and a polyglot[^6], whereby `compose.mk` is simultaneously a Makefile, a Makefile library, *and* a bash script.  

The way this works is as follows:
:  The bash script invokes `make`, but wraps it to catch any signals that are thrown.  After throwing a supported signal causes `make` to exit, bash catches, and then passes the code for the caught signal back to *another* invocation of `make` for an exit handler.

**As a consequence of this, signals can only be handled when `compose.mk` is invoked directly,** i.e. with `./compose.mk ...`, and cannot work for `make -f compose.mk`.  

The [pre/post hooks]({{_}}/runtime/#pre-post-hooks) have exactly the same limitation, but a different implementation.  Since the bash wrapper-script ultimately controls the initial invocation of `make`, it's possible to dynamically inject targets *between* any targets that are initially declared.

### For Developers
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

All targets related to signals/supervisors can be found under the `mk.interrupt` and `mk.super` namespaces, respectively.  These targets are for low-level internal use, and even hacking directly on `compose.mk` source probably won't require editing them, considering the [other techniques available](#inheritance-interpreters).  Quick links to the relevant parts of the API are collected in the [See Also](#see-also) section below.

